<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Writing Assistants</span>
      <h1>Grammarly vs ProWritingAid: Which Writing Assistant Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Grammarly and ProWritingAid are the two writing assistants most often shortlisted for serious work in 2026, and they are not interchangeable. Grammarly wins on real-time corrections, broad platform support, and everyday business writing. ProWritingAid wins on deep structural analysis, long-form editing reports, and value for professional writers. This comparison breaks down grammar accuracy, style suggestions, plagiarism, pricing, integrations, and long-form editing so you can pick the right one for your workflow.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.grammarly.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Grammarly Free</a>
      <a href="https://prowritingaid.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try ProWritingAid Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Grammarly</th><th>ProWritingAid</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Everyday writing, business, real-time corrections</td><td>Long-form, fiction, deep structural editing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Yes, grammar and spelling only</td><td>Yes, limited reports</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Premium Price</td><td>$12/mo (annual) or $30/mo (monthly)</td><td>$10/mo (annual) or $20/mo (monthly)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lifetime Plan</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, $399 one-time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Real-time Suggestions</td><td>Best in class, instant</td><td>Good, but slightly slower</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Number of Reports</td><td>Basic style and grammar</td><td>20+ detailed reports</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plagiarism Checker</td><td>Yes, web and ProQuest academic</td><td>Yes, web only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Writing Features</td><td>GrammarlyGO (generative AI)</td><td>Limited AI suggestions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Browser Extension</td><td>Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge</td><td>Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desktop Apps</td><td>Windows, Mac</td><td>Windows, Mac</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile Apps</td><td>iOS, Android (keyboard)</td><td>iOS only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Microsoft Word Integration</td><td>Native add-in</td><td>Native add-in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Google Docs Integration</td><td>Native add-on</td><td>Native add-on</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scrivener Integration</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tone Detection</td><td>Yes, real-time</td><td>Limited</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Grammarly?</h2>
    <p>Grammarly is an AI-powered writing assistant founded in 2009 by Max Lytvyn, Alex Shevchenko, and Dmytro Lider. It launched publicly in 2011 and has grown to over 30 million daily active users as of 2026, making it the most widely used writing assistant in the world. Grammarly was originally focused on catching grammar and spelling mistakes, but it has since expanded into style suggestions, tone detection, clarity rewrites, and generative AI through GrammarlyGO.</p>
    <p>The current version supports a browser extension that works on nearly every website, native apps for Windows and Mac, mobile keyboards for iOS and Android, and integrations with Microsoft Word, Google Docs, Slack, and Apple Mail. Grammarly Premium includes advanced grammar, clarity, engagement, delivery, and plagiarism detection. Grammarly Business adds team style guides, brand tones, and analytics. Pricing starts at free, Premium at $12 per month annually, and Business at $15 per member per month.</p>

    <h2>What Is ProWritingAid?</h2>
    <p>ProWritingAid is a writing assistant founded in 2013 by Chris Banks. It was built specifically for long-form writers, novelists, and editors who need deeper analysis than basic grammar checking. ProWritingAid's distinguishing feature is its 20+ detailed reports that analyze pacing, dialogue, overused words, repetitive sentence structures, sticky sentences, and consistency across a full manuscript.</p>
    <p>The current version supports a web editor, desktop apps for Windows and Mac, browser extensions, and integrations with Microsoft Word, Google Docs, and Scrivener. ProWritingAid Premium includes all 20+ reports, the desktop app, and integrations. ProWritingAid Premium Pro adds plagiarism checks. Pricing starts at free with limited reports, Premium at $10 per month annually, Premium Pro at $12 per month, and a lifetime license at $399. ProWritingAid is the choice of many NaNoWriMo participants, traditionally published novelists, and freelance editors.</p>

    <h2>Grammar Accuracy and Spell Check</h2>
    <p>Grammar accuracy is the foundation of any writing assistant. Both Grammarly and ProWritingAid catch common grammar and spelling mistakes, but Grammarly is faster and more accurate in real-time.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Grammar Engine</h3>
    <p>Grammarly's grammar engine is the best in the category. It catches subject-verb agreement, article errors, comma splices, dangling modifiers, and homophone confusion with high accuracy. Its real-time suggestions appear as you type, with no perceptible lag, and the explanations are clear and educational. Grammarly also handles context-sensitive corrections better than ProWritingAid, so it knows when "affect" should be "effect" based on the surrounding sentence.</p>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Grammar Engine</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid's grammar engine is solid but slightly less accurate than Grammarly. It catches the same categories of errors but occasionally misses nuanced cases or flags correct constructions as errors. The real-time suggestions are slightly slower than Grammarly, which matters when you are writing quickly. ProWritingAid compensates with detailed explanations that link to style guides, which is valuable for writers who want to learn the rules rather than just fix the mistake.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Grammarly</span> wins grammar accuracy and real-time spell check.</p>

    <h2>Style and Clarity Suggestions</h2>
    <p>Style suggestions help you write more clearly and engagingly. Grammarly is better for quick fixes, while ProWritingAid is better for deep analysis.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Style</h3>
    <p>Grammarly Premium flags wordiness, passive voice, unclear antecedents, and overly complex sentences. Its clarity rewrites suggest shorter alternatives, and its engagement suggestions flag monotonous sentence structure. GrammarlyGO, the generative AI layer, can rewrite full sentences in a different tone or length. For business writers who want quick, actionable style fixes, Grammarly is the more practical tool.</p>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Style Reports</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid's style analysis is far deeper. The Style Report flags overused words, repetitive sentence starts, sticky sentences, and pacing issues. The Echo Report detects repeated words or phrases within a paragraph. The Echo Report and the Pacing Report, which analyzes sentence length variation, are unique to ProWritingAid and invaluable for novelists. For writers who want to understand and improve their style at a structural level, ProWritingAid is the better tool.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best quick fixes go to <span class="winner-tag">Grammarly</span>. Best deep analysis goes to <span class="winner-tag">ProWritingAid</span>.</p>

    <h2>Long-Form Editing and Manuscript Analysis</h2>
    <p>For novels, scripts, and long-form content, deep manuscript analysis matters. ProWritingAid is the clear leader here.</p>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Manuscript Reports</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid offers 20+ reports designed for long-form work. The Consistency Report checks character names, place names, and capitalization across a manuscript. The Dialogue Report analyzes dialogue tags and suggests alternatives to "said." The Overused Words Report flags words you rely on too often. The Alliteration Report and the Echo Report catch unintentional repetition. The Pacing Report visualizes sentence length variation, which helps writers control rhythm in fiction. No other writing assistant offers this depth.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Long-Form</h3>
    <p>Grammarly handles long-form documents but treats them as a sequence of sentences. It does not analyze consistency across a full manuscript, does not track character names, and does not visualize pacing. For a 100,000-word novel, Grammarly catches grammar and style issues sentence by sentence, but it misses the structural problems that matter most to fiction writers.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">ProWritingAid</span> wins long-form editing and manuscript analysis.</p>

    <h2>Plagiarism Detection</h2>
    <p>For academic and publishing work, plagiarism detection is essential. Grammarly has the stronger database.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Plagiarism</h3>
    <p>Grammarly's plagiarism checker compares your text against 16 billion web pages and ProQuest academic databases. It is included with Grammarly Premium and Grammarly Business. The checker is fast, and the results page highlights matching text with source links. For students and academics, Grammarly's ProQuest integration is a meaningful advantage over competitors.</p>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Plagiarism</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid's plagiarism checker is available only on Premium Pro plans and checks against web sources only. It does not include academic databases. The checker is functional but less comprehensive than Grammarly's. For casual plagiarism checks, it is sufficient, but for academic or publishing use, Grammarly is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Grammarly</span> wins plagiarism detection.</p>

    <h2>AI Writing Features</h2>
    <p>Generative AI is now a core feature of writing tools. Grammarly has a clear lead here.</p>

    <h3>GrammarlyGO</h3>
    <p>GrammarlyGO is a generative AI layer that can draft emails, rewrite sentences in a different tone, shorten or expand text, and generate ideas. It is integrated directly into the Grammarly editor and browser extension, so you can invoke it without switching tools. GrammarlyGO uses a combination of proprietary models and licensed LLMs, and it inherits Grammarly's tone and style guidance. For business users who want AI drafting inside their existing workflow, GrammarlyGO is the most integrated solution.</p>

    <h3>ProWritingAid AI</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid's AI features are limited. It offers AI-powered rephrase suggestions and an AI spark feature for brainstorming, but it does not have a full generative AI layer comparable to GrammarlyGO. ProWritingAid focuses on analysis and editing rather than generation. For writers who want AI to help them write, Grammarly is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> <span class="winner-tag">Grammarly</span> wins AI writing features.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing depends on whether you pay monthly, annually, or want a lifetime license. ProWritingAid is cheaper overall.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Grammarly</th><th>ProWritingAid</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>Grammar and spelling only</td><td>Limited reports</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Monthly</td><td>$30/mo (Premium)</td><td>$20/mo (Premium)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annual</td><td>$144/yr ($12/mo)</td><td>$120/yr ($10/mo)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2-Year</td><td>$240 ($10/mo)</td><td>$240 ($10/mo)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lifetime</td><td>Not available</td><td>$399 one-time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plagiarism Included</td><td>Yes (Premium)</td><td>Premium Pro only ($12/mo)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Business Plan</td><td>$15/member/mo</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Team Style Guides</td><td>Yes (Business)</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Generative AI</td><td>GrammarlyGO included</td><td>Limited AI suggestions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refund Window</td><td>10 days</td><td>14 days</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For annual subscribers, ProWritingAid Premium at $120 per year is cheaper than Grammarly Premium at $144 per year. The lifetime plan from ProWritingAid at $399 is the best long-term value, paying for itself in under 3 years compared to annual plans.</p>
    <p>For teams, Grammarly Business at $15 per member per month is the only option, as ProWritingAid does not offer a team plan. For business use, Grammarly is the default choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best individual value goes to <span class="winner-tag">ProWritingAid</span>. Best team plan goes to <span class="winner-tag">Grammarly</span>.</p>

    <h2>Platform and Integration Support</h2>
    <p>Where you write matters as much as what the tool catches. Grammarly has broader platform support.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Integrations</h3>
    <p>Grammarly's browser extension works on nearly every website, including Gmail, Google Docs, WordPress, LinkedIn, Twitter, Slack, and Notion. Native apps for Windows and Mac let you edit documents offline. The mobile keyboard works on iOS and Android, correcting grammar in text messages, emails, and social posts. Microsoft Word and Google Docs integrations are native and seamless.</p>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Integrations</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid has browser extensions, desktop apps, and integrations with Microsoft Word, Google Docs, and Scrivener. It does not have a mobile keyboard, and its iOS app is limited. The Scrivener integration is a major advantage for novelists who write in Scrivener, as it is the only major writing assistant that supports it natively. For writers who work across many apps, Grammarly is more convenient, but for Scrivener users, ProWritingAid is the only choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best broad platform support goes to <span class="winner-tag">Grammarly</span>. Best Scrivener support goes to <span class="winner-tag">ProWritingAid</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Grammarly Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Best real-time grammar and spell check in the category</li>
        <li>GrammarlyGO generative AI integrated into the editor</li>
        <li>Broadest platform support including mobile keyboards</li>
        <li>Plagiarism checker includes ProQuest academic database</li>
        <li>Business plan with team style guides and analytics</li>
        <li>Real-time tone detection and clarity rewrites</li>
        <li>Native integrations with Word, Google Docs, and Slack</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Grammarly Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>More expensive than ProWritingAid at annual tier</li>
        <li>No lifetime plan available</li>
        <li>Limited deep structural analysis for long-form</li>
        <li>No Scrivener integration</li>
        <li>No pacing or dialogue reports for fiction</li>
        <li>Business plan required for team features</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>20+ detailed reports for long-form and fiction editing</li>
        <li>Lifetime plan at $399 for long-term value</li>
        <li>Cheaper annual plan at $120 per year</li>
        <li>Native Scrivener integration for novelists</li>
        <li>Consistency, pacing, and dialogue reports unique to the category</li>
        <li>Detailed explanations linked to style guides</li>
        <li>Desktop app for offline editing</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>ProWritingAid Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Real-time suggestions slower than Grammarly</li>
        <li>No mobile keyboard for iOS or Android</li>
        <li>Limited AI writing features compared to GrammarlyGO</li>
        <li>Plagiarism checker is web-only and requires Premium Pro</li>
        <li>No business or team plan</li>
        <li>Interface feels less polished than Grammarly</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Business Professionals and Everyday Writers</h3>
      <p>If you write emails, reports, and documents daily and want quick, accurate corrections across every app you use, Grammarly is the better choice. Its real-time suggestions, broad platform support, and GrammarlyGO AI layer make it the most practical daily writing tool. The Premium plan at $12 per month is reasonable for the time it saves on proofreading.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Novelists and Fiction Writers</h3>
      <p>If you write long-form fiction, novels, or scripts, ProWritingAid is the better choice. Its 20+ reports analyze pacing, dialogue, consistency, and overused words across a full manuscript, which Grammarly cannot do. The Scrivener integration is essential for novelists who write in Scrivener, and the lifetime plan at $399 is the best value for writers who will use the tool for years.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Academic and Student Writers</h3>
      <p>If you write academic papers, theses, or dissertations, Grammarly is the better choice. Its plagiarism checker includes the ProQuest academic database, which is essential for academic integrity. Its citation style suggestions and formal tone detection are also stronger than ProWritingAid's. The Premium plan at $144 per year is a reasonable investment for students who need rigorous plagiarism checking.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Freelance Editors and Content Teams</h3>
      <p>If you edit other people's work or manage a content team, Grammarly Business is the better choice. Team style guides, brand tones, and analytics let you enforce consistency across a team. ProWritingAid has no team plan, so it is better suited to individual editors who want deep analysis on each manuscript they edit.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Non-Native English Writers</h3>
      <p>If English is your second language, Grammarly is the better choice. Its real-time corrections, clear explanations, and context-sensitive grammar engine catch mistakes that non-native speakers commonly make. GrammarlyGO can also rephrase awkward sentences into natural English. ProWritingAid is more useful for native speakers who want to refine their style.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Editing a 100,000-word novel</h3>
      <p>A novelist finishes a 100,000-word manuscript and needs a final edit pass. ProWritingAid's Consistency Report catches that a character named "Catherine" is spelled "Katherine" in chapter 12. The Pacing Report shows that chapter 7 has monotonous sentence lengths. The Dialogue Report flags overuse of "said" and suggests alternatives. The Overused Words Report shows "look" appears 147 times. The novelist revises based on the reports and produces a tighter manuscript. Grammarly would catch grammar errors but miss all of these structural issues.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Writing business emails on the go</h3>
      <p>A sales manager drafts client emails from their phone between meetings. Grammarly's mobile keyboard corrects grammar and tone in real-time, suggesting "I apologize for the delay" instead of "Sorry for the delay" for a formal client. GrammarlyGO rewrites a rambling paragraph into a concise three-sentence email. The manager sends polished emails without opening a separate app. ProWritingAid has no mobile keyboard, so this workflow is impossible.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Checking a thesis for plagiarism</h3>
      <p>A graduate student finishes a 15,000-word thesis and needs to verify originality before submission. Grammarly's plagiarism checker compares the text against 16 billion web pages and the ProQuest academic database, flagging a paragraph that closely matches a 2019 journal article. The student rewrites the paragraph and cites the source. ProWritingAid's web-only plagiarism checker would have missed the academic source.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Drafting a blog post with AI assistance</h3>
      <p>A content marketer needs to draft a 1,000-word blog post on a tight deadline. GrammarlyGO generates a first draft based on a prompt, then the marketer refines it with Grammarly's clarity and engagement suggestions. The final post is polished and on-brand in 20 minutes. ProWritingAid has no comparable generative AI feature, so the marketer would need to draft manually or use a separate AI tool.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best for everyday and business writing: Grammarly.</strong> Grammarly Premium at $12 per month delivers the best real-time grammar checking, broadest platform support, and integrated generative AI through GrammarlyGO. For business professionals, students, and non-native English writers who want quick, accurate corrections across every app, Grammarly is the practical choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for long-form and fiction: ProWritingAid.</strong> ProWritingAid Premium at $10 per month offers 20+ detailed reports that analyze pacing, dialogue, consistency, and overused words across a full manuscript. For novelists, scriptwriters, and freelance editors who need deep structural analysis, ProWritingAid is the only tool that does this well. The lifetime plan at $399 is the best long-term value.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for academic and plagiarism: Grammarly.</strong> Grammarly's plagiarism checker includes the ProQuest academic database, which is essential for students and academics. Its formal tone detection and citation style suggestions are also stronger than ProWritingAid's.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> Grammarly Premium at $12 per month for daily writing and real-time corrections, plus ProWritingAid Premium at $10 per month for long-form manuscript analysis. Total cost is $22 per month or $264 per year, which is reasonable for professional writers and editors who need both quick fixes and deep analysis.</p>
    </div>

