    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Grammarly better than ProWritingAid for most writers?</h3>
    <p>Grammarly is better for everyday writers, business professionals, and non-native English speakers who want quick, accurate corrections across email, documents, and social media. Its real-time suggestions and broad platform support make it the more practical daily tool. ProWritingAid is better for long-form writers, novelists, and editors who need deep structural analysis and detailed reports.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Grammarly or ProWritingAid?</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid is cheaper at the annual tier. ProWritingAid Premium costs $120 per year, while Grammarly Premium costs $144 per year. Monthly pricing is closer, with Grammarly at $30 per month and ProWritingAid at $20 per month. The lifetime plan from ProWritingAid at $399 is the best long-term value, while Grammarly does not offer a lifetime option.</p>

    <h3>Does Grammarly or ProWritingAid have better plagiarism checking?</h3>
    <p>Grammarly has the more comprehensive plagiarism database, drawing from both web sources and academic publications through ProQuest. ProWritingAid's plagiarism checker is included in its Premium plan but has a smaller database and is web-only. For academic and publishing use, Grammarly's plagiarism detection is more thorough.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Grammarly and ProWritingAid together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and some writers do. A common workflow is to draft in Grammarly for real-time corrections and surface-level fixes, then run a ProWritingAid report on the final draft for deeper structural analysis, pacing, and overused word detection. The combined annual cost is $264, which is reasonable for professional writers and editors.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for long-form fiction and novels?</h3>
    <p>ProWritingAid is the better choice for long-form fiction and novels. Its 20+ reports analyze pacing, dialogue tags, repetitive sentence starts, overused words, and consistency in character names. Grammarly focuses more on correctness and clarity, which works for shorter pieces but misses the structural issues that matter in a 100,000-word novel.</p>

    <h3>Which has better integrations with other writing apps?</h3>
    <p>Grammarly has broader integrations, with native support in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, web browsers, iOS, Android, and desktop apps. ProWritingAid integrates with Word, Google Docs, Scrivener, and browser extensions, but its desktop app and platform coverage are narrower. For writers who work across many apps, Grammarly is more convenient.</p>

